{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Writing an E2E smoke worth trusting, and the retry policy that protects it</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A smoke test earns its keep by being reliable, not by being thorough. Keep it to the single happy path, select elements by role or an explicit test attribute rather than CSS classes that change for unrelated reasons, and avoid arbitrary waits, wait for the actual state the next step depends on. Resist the urge to bolt extra assertions onto it "since it's already there," that's the exact mechanism that turns one trustworthy smoke test back into a slow, flaky suite.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Run the smoke against a real, staging-like environment, not one where every downstream dependency is mocked, mocking the payment provider in the one test whose entire purpose is verifying the real integration defeats it. And protect the test's credibility with policy, not just code: a failing E2E smoke should never be silently retried into a merge; treat a flaky result the same as a real one, quarantine and fix it immediately, because a smoke test nobody trusts is worse than no smoke test, it creates false confidence instead of an honest gap.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This scales past "just one test" as a product genuinely grows more critical paths, sign-up, checkout, and say, subscription cancellation, might each earn their own smoke, but the discipline stays the same: each one is chosen because a silent failure there is unambiguous and costly, not because a feature exists. The moment E2E count starts tracking feature count instead of criticality count, the suite is drifting back toward Team B's failure mode, and it's worth periodically auditing the E2E suite specifically for tests that could be demoted to integration coverage without losing real confidence.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

